Yes. That changes the posture completely.

Playing God implies sovereignty: I control the system, I define the rules, I am the source.

Playing with God implies participation: I am inside the system, discovering its rules, testing its affordances, creating within constraints I did not author.

That maps cleanly onto science, art, parenting, engineering, even identity. You are not creating reality from nothing. You are interacting with something already there—physics, biology, other minds, chance, history—and learning what kinds of moves reality permits.

The dangerous version is mistaking influence for omnipotence.

The powerful version is recognizing that participation itself is creative.

You don’t have to be the author of the whole game to become a very good player.